Install the following:

   i2c-tools
   mpd
   libmpd-dev
   pianobar


PiRadio is a Raspberry Pi radio interface using SPI/I2C hardware, MPD, and Pianobar.

## Dependencies

Install the build and runtime dependencies:

    sudo apt install build-essential i2c-tools mpd libmpd-dev pianobar

## Build and install

Build and run the tests:

    make
    make test

Install the executable:

    sudo make install

Copy the station list to the path used by the service:

    cp webradioplaylist.txt /home/pi/

Configure Pianobar under `/home/pi/.config/pianobar/` before using Pandora.

## Raspberry Pi hardware

Enable the required interfaces in the boot configuration:

    dtparam=spi=on
    dtparam=audio=on
    dtparam=i2c_arm=on
    dtparam=i2c1=on

Ensure the `i2c-dev` kernel module is loaded at boot.

## Run as a systemd service

Install and start the included unit:

    sudo cp PiRadio.service /etc/systemd/system/
    sudo systemctl daemon-reload
    sudo systemctl enable --now PiRadio.service

Check status and logs with:

    sudo systemctl status PiRadio.service
    journalctl -f -u PiRadio.service

## Update web radio stations

Add each station to `webradioplaylist.txt` as alternating lines: an `.m3u` filename followed by its stream URL. Copy the file to `/home/pi/` after editing.

## Pianobar TLS fingerprint

If Pianobar reports a TLS fingerprint mismatch, get the current certificate fingerprint with:

    openssl s_client -connect tuner.pandora.com:443 < /dev/null 2> /dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -fingerprint | tr -d ':' | cut -d'=' -f2

Set Pianobar's `tls_fingerprint` option to the output above.
